<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uicheon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1055–1101</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uicheon was a Korean Buddhist monk of the Goryeo period and the fourth son of King Munjong. After studying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1085-1086, he collected Buddhist commentaries from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and had them printed as a supplement to the Tripitaka. In 1097 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheontae">Cheontae</a> school, seeking to unite doctrinal study and meditation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uicheon.jpg" alt="Uicheon" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 도일비구(道日比丘) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uicheon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
